;; TEST-EXPECT: PASS
;; SKIP-WITH[--single-pass]: "interprocedural uniformity inference needs the multi-pass call graph; single-pass compiles clampish before its call site (clampish (~ x)) exists, so its parameter can't be inferred uniform and the if+ is rejected"
;;
;; Endeavor 124 A2 — BLOCKERS (a)+(c): an int sub-function whose body uses if+.
;; This is 120/03's `C`, standalone (called directly from a kernel, not through
;; another sub-fn), and with `int` instead of `ulong` to keep (b) out of it.
;;
;; clampish(x) = (if+ (< x 13) x (- x 2))     dclampish/dx = 1 in both branches.
;;
;; This one fails FORWARD too (not just --differentiate), which was the point:
;; `if+` drags in the whole UNIFORMITY subsystem, and that entanglement is a big
;; part of why 120/03 was the hard case. FIXED 2026-07-01 (blocker (c)) — with NO
;; (declare (uniform)): interprocedural uniformity inference now proves that a
;; scalar kernel input threaded through the call is uniform. Two clauses were
;; added to the pre-pass walker %uni-analyze (src/analysis/core.lisp):
;;   - `~` (memory read): (~ src [idx]) is uniform iff its handle and index are
;;     uniform (taint-max). A whole-cell read (~ x) has no index -> just SRC.
;;   - `marshall-*`: the kernel explodes a cell param into (ptr size offset) and
;;     reassembles it via (let ((x (marshall-cell TYPE size ptr off))) ...); the
;;     marshalled handle is uniform iff its RUNTIME args are (leading type skipped).
;; Together these let x_ptr (uniform kernel arg) -> marshall-cell -> (~ x) flow
;; :uniform into clampish's parameter, so its if+ proves uniform in BOTH forward
;; and the recomputed backward. (blocker (a) int registration was fixed by the
;; activity analysis in 07.) IR-verified: clampish_grad(i32,float) does
;; x_adj += seed in both branches -> dclampish/dx = 1.

(def-function clampish (x)
  (declare #'(int => int))
  (if+ (< x 13) x (- x 2)))

(def-type in-i  (cell int :address-space :global))
(def-type out-i (cell int :address-space :global))

(def-kernel use_clampish (x &out m)
  (declare #'(in-i &out out-i))
  (set! (~ m) (clampish (~ x))))
